<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.4: Using the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of Quadratics</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01918</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.4: Using the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of Quadratics</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7b84c419-79ef-47ee-9b50-cd7f4bc59da6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (15 minutes)</title>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Prior to the activity, ask students to volunteer different key characteristics of quadratics that have already been used in the lesson. Examples include: \(x\)-intercepts, zeros, solutions, vertex, maximum, minimum. Collect these words on the board for all to refer to with a diagram of a quadratic.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>While answering the questions below, plot each point on a coordinate plane with grid paper by hand. Do not use technology.</para>
<para>For questions 1–4, use the the function \(f(x)=(x-2)(x+4).\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the \(x\)-coordinates of the \(x\)-intercepts of \(f(x)\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=-4\) and \(x =2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the coordinate of the vertex. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((-1, -5)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> On your graph, sketch a quadratic through the key features on your graph and draw a vertical line through the vertex using a different color.  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> </para>
<para>Compare your answer: </para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7c569cfd15ffb1afb73d2259d8906b0d96741eb8"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the equation of the vertical line that goes through the vertex? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=-1\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> What does the vertical line that goes through the vertex do to the parabola? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The vertical line \(x= -1\) cuts the parabola in half and creates two symmetrical parts.</para>
<para>This imaginary vertical line that goes through the vertex and cuts the parabola in half is called the <term data-store="glossary-tooltip" data-schema-version="1.0">axis of symmetry</term>.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> On your graph, label your \(x\)-intercepts, vertex, and axis of symmetry. Identify your vertex as a maximum or minimum. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> </para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2355010bb11c4a259c69fb1b8f261912d62bbcfb"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> Given a vertex of \((2, -4)\) and a point \((1, 3)\), what is another point on the quadratic? </item>
</list>
<para>(Hint: It is helpful to draw a picture and use the axis of symmetry).</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. </para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d62313b400bbc8ca6fcb49bcdae93601a6a71a5f"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Since \((-2, 4)\) is the vertex, then \(x=-2\) is the axis of symmetry. If \((1, 3)\) is 3 units away from the axis of symmetry, there is another point 3 units to the left of the axis of symmetry. This is the point \((-5, 3)\).</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Focus the discussion on how students determined the key characteristics of the graph.</para>
<para>Ask questions like:</para>
<list>
  <item> “How did you find the \(x\)-intercepts without using a graph? (Using the factored form, set each factor equal to 0 and solve). </item>
  <item> “How did you find the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex? (Find the halfway point between the \(x\)-intercepts).  </item>
  <item> “How did you find the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex? (Substitute the \(x\)-value of the vertex into the function).  </item>
  <item> How can you now find the axis of symmetry? (The equation is the same as the \(x\)-value of the vertex).  </item>
  <item> How can knowing the axis of symmetry help in graphing? (One possible answer should be that there will always be a point on either side of the parabola equidistant from the axis of symmetry, so if students know one point, they can plot another). </item>
</list>

<title>7.11.4: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>What is the equation of the axis of symmetry for \(f(x)=(x+1)(x-3)\)?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">ANSWERS</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">FEEDBACK</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x=-1\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This is one of the \(x\)-intercepts. The answer is \(x=1\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x = 3\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This is one of the \(x\)-intercepts. The answer is \(x=1.\)</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x=1\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: The axis of symmetry is the same as the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex. The \(x\)-intercepts are at \(x = -1\) and \(x=3\) so the vertex is halfway between at \(x = 1\). This is also the equation of the axis of symmetry.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x=-4\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex. The answer is \(x=1\).</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title> 7.11.4: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>

<title>Axis of Symmetry of a Quadratic </title>
<para>In the graph of a quadratic function, the axis of symmetry is an imaginary line that cuts the parabola in half to create two symmetrical sides. It goes through the vertex of the parabola so the equation of the axis of symmetry is the same as the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.</para>
<para><emphasis id="docs-internal-guid-1a59f64e-7fff-f686-b37e-3c95d10108a1"><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7e89a7ce1c5f5104584a662b6f26582bde6ab96a"/></media></figure></emphasis></para>
<para>Notice that in Figure 1, the vertex is at \((-2, -2)\) and the axis of symmetry is at \(x= -2\).</para>
<para>In Figure 2, the vertex is at \((2, 7)\) and the axis of symmetry is at \(x =2\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<para>Find the vertex and axis of symmetry for \(f(x)=(x+2)(x-6)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> -  Find the \(x\)-intercepts.
  Set each factor equal to 0.
  \(x+2=0\)
  \(x=-2\)
  \(x-6=0\)
  \(x=6\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.
  The vertex is halfway between the two \(x\)-intercepts.
  \(x=6+(-2)^2=2\)
  \(x = \frac{6+(-2)}{2} =2\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 </emphasis>- Find the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex.
  Substitute the \(x\)-coordinate into \(f(x)\).
  \(f(2)=(2+2)(2-6)=(4)(-4)=-6\)
  The vertex is at \((2, -16)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Find the axis of symmetry.
  The axis of symmetry is the equation where \(x\) equals the \(x\)-coordinate of the function.
  \(x= 2\)</para>
  
<title>Try It: Axis of Symmetry of a Quadratic</title>
<para>For questions 1 - 2, use the equation of the quadratic, \(f(x)=(x-4)(x-10)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the vertex of the quadratic?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((7, -9)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the \(x\)-intercepts.
  Set each factor equal to 0.
  \(x-4=0\)
  \(x=4\)
  \(x-10=0\)
  \(x=10\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.
  The vertex is halfway between the two \(x\)-intercepts.
  \(x=\frac{4+10}{2}=7\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Find the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex.
  Substitute the \(x\)-coordinate into \(f(x)\).
  \(f(7)=(7-4)(7-10)=(3)(-3)=-9\)
  The vertex is at \((7, -9)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the axis of symmetry of the quadratic? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=7\)</para>
<para>The axis of symmetry is the equation where \(x\) equals the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.
  \(x= 7\)</para>
</section>
</content>
